A||N||16||murray||Oxygen Sensor||||1||
Z||000000||murray||08-26-2001||06:55 PM||||I have an 87 SE 2.5 I am trying to replace an oxygen sensor on, The old one came out fine but the new one, Bosch 12014 is too small to screw in, Help!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-26-2001||07:12 PM||||Last one I did... treads are the same on the bosch but you need a smaller wrench. if you mean the treads are small then you got the wrong sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||08-26-2001||07:22 PM||||Bosch #12014 is the # on the box for my 84 4cyl. Parts person probably looked up wrong year if that makes a difference. <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||murray||08-26-2001||07:50 PM||||The part seems to be the correct part number but the replacement sensor is too small a diameter to fit ||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||08-26-2001||08:07 PM||||What size wrench fits the old one? The correct 02 takes a 22mm wrench I believe.Did the old one have a bushing on it maybe? Look on the old one and see if you can find a manufactorer name & possibly a #. It is possible the original bore was stripped out or cracked and repaired by overtapping, or that it isn't a stock exhaust. Don't know any other options to suggest, except to take the old one to the parts store and get them to match it up. Let us know how it works out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||murray||08-26-2001||08:28 PM||||thanks for the advice,  part # is 8344 no manufacturer, the car is has a dual exhaust that appears to be factory, could that make the difference?  the front and rear of the car is like a GT, not like most SEs I've seen, could SEs be ordered that way? Thanks again||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||08-26-2001||09:30 PM||||Murray, you have mail||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||08-27-2001||09:33 AM||||Fortunately (or unfortunately, depending upon your perspective), I've never had to remove my O2 sensor. Nearest I ever came was when I removed the manifold to have a crack welded. Tried in vain to remove the O2. The welder said not to. He could weld it with the O2 in place.<BR>I believe the 87 and 88 exhaust manifolds are different than the previous years. I'll look in the 22P book, tonight and see if the O2 sensors are the same, or not. You might have been sold the wrong part. <P>Hate to bring this up, but is it possible that the fitting stripped out when you removed the O2? I've heard of it happening on the V-6 cars. If not, then you've likely got the wrong part.<P>The dual exhaust (nice!) should have no bearing on the situation.<P>Oh... Welcome to the Forum! <BR>Insanity runs rampant. We like it here.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Songman||08-27-2001||10:18 AM||||I replaced the oxygen sensor on my 88 coupe last year and it went off without a hitch. Got the part from Advance Auto and had the old one out and the new one in inside of 10 minutes.<P>I think I might check with another auto parts store to see if you did get the wrong part number. Oxygen sensor change should be a very easy thing.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fieroburton||08-27-2001||12:57 PM||||can you pass a e-check with a bad oxygen sencor ?<BR>just wondering ||||reg||5
Z||000010||Formula88||08-27-2001||02:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroburton:<BR><B>can you pass a e-check with a bad oxygen sencor ?<BR>just wondering </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Probably not.  The ECM will default to an over-rich mode to prevent engine damage if it's not getting a signal from the O2 sensor.  That'll make you blow your smog test.  If the O2 sensor is just on it's way out, but still working (i.e. no codes yet) you may pass.   ||||reg||1
Z||000011||maryjane||08-27-2001||09:11 PM||||He has the correct Bosch 02 sensor. I just changed mine in eary July and still have the box. #12014. He is saying the od of the sensor is too small for the id of the threaded bore in the exh. Maybe someone switched the parts in the box, I dunno.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Primaris||08-28-2001||12:34 AM||||Sounds like you have an after market exhaust.  Hooker use to make one.  Take the old O2 sensor to your parts store and get a match.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000013||BlackDragon||08-28-2001||12:40 AM||||murray, where are you located....just out of curiosity<P>------------------<BR>You pull up to a stop light..........you hear an engine rev....you look over and see a hard core, riced out, shogun, eurostriped, 3 level spoiler, civic.....you think to yourself "this should be fun"......you rev your engine.....the light turns green, you pop the clutch......shift to 2nd........ 3rd....... 4th.... you are winning, then you smell it and realize something bad has happened..... you look over at your best friend in the passenger seat, he looks back and you say "damnit dude, why did you have to fart" :damon:||||reg||1
Z||000014||72vinman||08-28-2001||01:11 AM||||If your O2 sensor is gone you will not pass the emissions test. I failed my first test with so called lethal levels of carbon. My reading was 2.96. To pass I think here you have to be below .76.  All i did was replace the O2 sensor with a new Bosch sensor and passed the test with a reading of .01. It only took a few minutes to change. They must have had the wrong sensor in the correct box.||||reg||10
Z||000015||Jhonny||08-28-2001||02:08 AM||||Our cars has 18mm spark plug thread for the<BR>oxygen sensor and that goes for both types<BR>of engines. <BR>Also 12mm threads exist for some cars but not<BR>for the Fiero.<P>/// Jhonny<P>------------------<BR>Jhonny Sokura<BR>Vadstena, Östergötland Sweden<P><A HREF="http://home.swipnet.se/jks/fiero3.jpg" TARGET=_blank> Pontiac Fiero GT -87 , V6 5spd<BR> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Cozmo||08-28-2001||01:40 PM||||My O2 sensor literally blew out of the downpipe on my '87 Duke, taking the threads in the bung with it (caused by a plugged cat). I just rigged up a clamp to hold the new O2 sensor in place, it's been holding and not leaking for 10K miles now...<BR>I know, a real kluge but IT WORKS..... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>BobC||||reg||1
